Day 38: Notes App (Write, Read, Append)

Concepts used: file modes "w", "a", "r", .readlines(), confirmation before destructive actions

Why this project?
On Day 36 you used "a" (append) and "r" (read). Today you'll use all three modes in one program, including "w", the dangerous one that erases the file before writing. A notes app is the best way to see the difference between them.
The three modes side by side
open("notes.txt", "w")   # write: erases everything, starts a fresh file
open("notes.txt", "a")   # append: keeps old content, adds at the end
open("notes.txt", "r")   # read: only reads, crashes if the file doesn't exist

A quick experiment to try in a separate file first:
with open("test.txt", "w") as file:
    file.write("First\n")
with open("test.txt", "w") as file:
    file.write("Second\n")

Run it, then cat test.txt. It will only show Second, because the second "w" erased First. Now change the second one to "a" and run it again from scratch. This time both lines survive.

Problem Statement
Build a notes app that stores everything in notes.txt. Menu:
1. Write New Notes (replaces everything)
2. Add a Note
3. Read Notes
4. Exit

Write New Notes: ask the user to type a note, then save it using "w" mode. Because this erases all old notes, ask Are you sure? (y/n) first.
Add a Note: save one more note at the end of the file using "a" mode.
Read Notes: show every note with a number in front.
Exit: quit the program.
Example Run
Choose: 2
Enter your note: Buy milk
Note added!

Choose: 2
Enter your note: Call Arun
Note added!

Choose: 3
Your Notes:
1. Buy milk
2. Call Arun

Choose: 1
This will erase all existing notes. Are you sure? (y/n): y
Enter your note: Start fresh
Notes replaced!

Choose: 3
Your Notes:
1. Start fresh

Close the program and run it again. Your notes should still be there.
Hints
Read with numbers. .readlines() gives a list of lines, and enumerate (from Day 20) numbers them:
with open("notes.txt", "r") as file:
    notes = file.readlines()
for number, note in enumerate(notes, start=1):
    print(f"{number}. {note.strip()}")

Write the confirmation first, then open the file in "w" mode only if the answer is y. The same idea as your address book delete in Day 30.

Things to handle
1.File doesn't exist yet: Read should print "No notes yet." instead of crashing (try/except FileNotFoundError, as on Day 36).
2.File exists but is empty: notes will be an empty list. Check len(notes) == 0 and print the same "No notes yet." message.
3.Empty note: don't save a note that is blank (note.strip() == "").


